  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nThe mid-level ridge centered south of the area and warm temperatures \naloft are keeping conditions very summer-like for the next few days \nbefore relief from the heat arrives. Highs today, like yesterday, \nwill reach the mid 90s with heat index values either side of 100 \ndegrees as dew points stay elevated. Tonight, an H5 trough axis \ncentered over the Great Basin and a surface boundary in Nebraska will\nnudge closer to the area. Low temps stay well above averages (which \nare now in the upper 50s) in the low 70s tonight. One more very hot \nday is then expected tomorrow before cooler weather settles in later\nthis weekend. \n\nThe cold front north of the area will start to move into northern KS \nmidday tomorrow, and continue southward through the afternoon.\nConvective initiation is likely near the boundary by mid/late \nafternoon, mainly east of a line from Seneca to Manhattan to Abilene.\nCoverage of storms is then expected to increase as a shortwave in \nthe southwesterly flow aloft arrives 00Z-03Z Sun. Before storms\ndevelop, instability will increase near and ahead of the boundary in\nour continued hot and moist environment. Inverted-v forecast \nsoundings from the HRRR and high DCAPE values would indicate that \ndamaging wind gusts will be a primary concern once storms form. Low \nlevel shear could be on the weaker side but deep layer shear should \nbe sufficient for organized updrafts that could also produce some \nquarter sized hail. Periods of heavy rain are also of concern since \nforecast pwat values of 2.0-2.2 inches exceeds the daily max of \nclimatology. Flooding risk remains fairly low, however, with training\nof storms not looking likely.\n\nThe surface boundary should be south of the area by early Sunday \nmorning. While POPs decrease through midday, additional scattered \nshower or storm development could occur Sunday afternoon/evening as \nanother shortwave moves over the area ahead of the main wave aloft. \nThe H85 front could also still be lingering in the area behind the \nsfc boundary. Instability will be more limited so severe weather is \nnot expected. \n\nThe main focus then becomes the much cooler weather that most of us \nwill begin to experience on Sunday. There could be a large gradient \ninitially on Sunday with highs in the 70s along and north of I-70, \nbut still in the mid 80s south of I-35. A surface ridge expands \nsouthward over the region early next week and temps cool aloft. \nLingering cloud cover will also help to keep conditions cool, \nresulting in low 70 highs Monday and possibly upper 60s (for highs!)\non Tuesday. Nighttime temps could be as cool as 50 degrees by \nTuesday night/early Wednesday. Temperatures stay below average all \nnext week with POPs increasing again toward the end of the week.",
